<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Augusta Raurica</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Augusta Raurica is a Roman town site at Augst near Basel, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>. A colony was founded in the land of the Raurici by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucius-munatius-plancus">Lucius Munatius Plancus</a> around 44 BCE, though the town itself grew up under Augustus from about 15 BCE. At its height it had perhaps 10,000 or more inhabitants, with a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a>, temples and baths; after the unrest of the 3rd century the fort of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaiseraugst">Castrum Rauracense</a> was built at nearby Kaiseraugst.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Augusta Raurica, or Colonia Augusta Rauracorum, stands on the south bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> about 20 km east of Basel, near the villages of Augst and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaiseraugst">Kaiseraugst</a>. It is the site of the oldest known Roman colony on the Rhine. The city lay on a high plateau just south of the river, where the small rivers Ergolz and Violen carved a triangle whose apex pointed north to the Rhine and held the Roman castrum. Steep slopes defended it to the north, east and west.</p><p>The colony's territory is estimated at around 700 km2, covering much of the modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangzhou">Canton</a> of Basel, the Frick valley and the eastern Jura of the Canton of Solothurn. It was one of three colonies bearing Augustus's name, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aosta">Augusta Praetoria</a> (Aosta) and Augusta Vindelicum (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augsburg">Augsburg</a>), which marked the corners of a triangle across his Alpine conquests.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucius-munatius-plancus">Lucius Munatius Plancus</a> founded the colony around 44 BCE among the Rauraci, a Gallic tribe related to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helvetii">Helvetii</a>. No archaeological evidence of that date has been found, which suggests either that the civil war after the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a> disturbed the settlement or that Plancus's colony lay in the area of modern Basel. Successful colonisation waited for Augustus's conquest of the central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a> around 15 BCE, and the oldest find at the site is dated to 6 BCE by dendrochronology. The city was laid out from the temple of Jupiter as its high point, on a grid of rectangular blocks of about 50 by 60 metres.</p><p>In the 2nd century the city was a prosperous trading centre, and it flourished between the 1st and 3rd centuries, exporting smoked pork and bacon to other parts of the empire. A powerful earthquake damaged much of it in 250 CE. Around 260 Alemanni tribes or marauding Roman troops destroyed it. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> built a fortress on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaiseraugst">Castrum Rauracense</a>, and resettled the city on a much smaller scale on its site. The two settlements are the centres of modern Augst and Kaiseraugst.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Augusta Raurica is an archaeological site and open-air museum. It had an amphitheatre, a main forum with a temple of Jupiter, an aqueduct, baths and the largest Roman theatre north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a>, seating 8,000 to 10,000. The site and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaiseraugst">Castrum Rauracense</a> are heritage sites of national significance. Some 80 per cent of the built-up area has yet to be excavated, and it is the best-preserved Roman city north of the Alps not built over in medieval or modern times. The Roman Museum holds the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> treasure of Kaiseraugst, found in the fortress in 1961–1962.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Augusta Raurica” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Around 44 BCE Lucius Munatius Plancus founded the colony of <b>Augusta Raurica</b> (Augst) in their land.</blockquote><a href="/state/raurici">Raurici</a></li></ul></section>
